Microsoft Learn: Configure Azure Container Instances

Platform: Microsoft Learn
Module: Configure Azure Container Instances
Track: AZ-104 Microsoft Azure Administrator
Difficulty: Intermediate
Date: 2026

Objective

Learn how to configure Azure Container Instances (ACI), understand how containers compare to virtual machines, implement container groups, and identify when Azure Container Apps may be a better solution.

This module focused on deploying lightweight containerized workloads in Azure without managing virtual machines, orchestration clusters, or underlying infrastructure.

Step 1: Understanding Containers

The module introduced containers as lightweight, portable application environments.

A container packages an application with everything it needs to run, including:

Application code
Runtime
Libraries
Dependencies
Configuration files
Key benefits:
Fast startup
Portability
Consistent deployments
Lower overhead than virtual machines
Easier application packaging

Containers help solve the problem of applications working differently across development, testing, and production environments.

Step 2: Containers vs Virtual Machines

The training compared containers with virtual machines.

Key differences:
Containers	Virtual Machines
Share the host operating system kernel	Include a full operating system
Lightweight	Heavier resource usage
Start quickly	Slower startup
Best for application packaging	Best for full OS control
More portable	More infrastructure control
Important lessons learned:
Virtual machines provide full operating system isolation
Containers are more lightweight and faster to deploy
Containers are ideal for microservices, APIs, batch jobs, and temporary workloads
VMs are better when full OS control or long-running server configuration is required

The module emphasized choosing the right compute option based on workload needs.

Step 3: Understanding Azure Container Instances

The module introduced Azure Container Instances as a serverless container hosting service.

Azure Container Instances allows administrators to run containers without managing:

Virtual machines
Kubernetes clusters
Operating system patches
Host infrastructure
Common use cases:
Simple containerized applications
Background jobs
Task automation
Build jobs
Development and testing
Short-lived workloads
Key benefits:
Fast container startup
Per-second billing
No infrastructure management
Simple deployment
Public or private networking options

Azure Container Instances is useful when an organization needs to run containers quickly without deploying a full orchestration platform.

Step 4: Implementing Azure Container Instances

The training covered how to deploy containers using Azure Container Instances.

Common configuration options:
Configuration	Purpose
Container image	Defines the application to run
CPU and memory	Allocates compute resources
Ports	Exposes application traffic
Environment variables	Passes configuration values
Restart policy	Controls container behavior after exit
Networking	Enables public or private access
Example workflow:
Container Image
      ↓
Azure Container Instance
      ↓
Running Application
Key lessons learned:
ACI runs container images from registries
CPU and memory are configured during deployment
Containers can expose ports for application access
Environment variables allow configuration without changing the image
Step 5: Understanding Container Groups

A major focus of the module was container groups.

A container group is a collection of containers that run together on the same host and share resources.

Container groups can share:
Network namespace
IP address
Ports
Volumes
Lifecycle
Example:
Container Group
├── Web App Container
└── Logging Sidecar Container
Benefits:
Supports multi-container workloads
Allows helper containers
Enables shared storage and networking
Simplifies related container deployment

The module emphasized that container groups are useful when containers must work closely together.

Step 6: Container Group Use Cases

The training demonstrated scenarios where container groups are useful.

Common patterns:
Main application plus logging sidecar
Web application plus monitoring container
Application plus helper process
Short-lived task plus supporting service
Key concepts learned:
Containers in the same group are scheduled together
They share the same lifecycle
They are suitable for tightly coupled services
They are not the same as Kubernetes orchestration

Container groups provide lightweight multi-container deployment without a full cluster.

Step 7: Understanding Azure Container Apps

The module introduced Azure Container Apps as another Azure container hosting option.

Azure Container Apps is designed for more advanced containerized applications.

Common capabilities:
Microservices hosting
Event-driven scaling
HTTP-based applications
Background processing
Dapr integration
Built-in scaling with KEDA
Azure Container Instances vs Azure Container Apps:
Azure Container Instances	Azure Container Apps
Simple container execution	Full app platform for containers
Best for short-lived or simple workloads	Best for microservices and scalable apps
Minimal orchestration	Built-in scaling and revisions
Direct container deployment	Application-focused container platform
Key lesson learned:

Use ACI for simple container execution and Azure Container Apps for more advanced application hosting needs.

